I agree with that. And I do like that touch too, Sean.

00:19:31.690 --> 00:19:39.630
That's a great point that you mentioned is that it's not can you you know, like you must go to the cave and defeat blah, blah, blah, and then defeat the monster in the cave.

00:19:39.770 --> 00:19:42.090
Go back and they're like, you must defeat the monster in the cave.

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:47.410
They do kind of evolve or at least you know, there's there's story checkpoints.

00:19:47.410 --> 00:19:56.790
And yes, like that also means that content will be gated off because you have to complete those story checkpoints before you can move forward.

00:19:56.790 --> 00:20:07.030
But it makes it feel much more like an adventure than, you know, like, like that you're helping out these towns and you're growing with these other characters.

00:20:07.030 --> 00:20:15.430
It makes the experience part of the game, the gaining experience points more than just a, you know, a grinding enemies thing.

00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:24.030
But you're also experiencing like the progress of this world that is currently being affected by Bob Morda.

00:20:25.270 --> 00:20:29.850
Yeah, it does a great job of like really contextualizing everything you need to do.

00:20:29.890 --> 00:20:36.530
It never feels like it's just giving you some vague weird hint that's just like, OK, obviously, they just want me to go to this next.

00:20:36.650 --> 00:20:43.670
It does feel like everything exists for a story purpose, even if it was probably there for a gameplay purpose.

00:20:43.970 --> 00:20:50.750
It just feels like they put thought into like, OK, what would the story of why you have to do this next be?

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:56.630
Like, so I never felt like I just was told what to do, but I always felt like I knew where to go because I was fouling the story.

00:20:57.070 --> 00:21:02.930
Whereas I feel like other games have sometimes there's been like those two elements have been sort of divorced from each other.

00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:12.630
And another thing to go back to the comparison with Zelda or Zelda likes is that, you know, there's not really there's no puzzle solving in this game, really.

00:21:12.630 --> 00:21:14.650
It comes down to the combat.

00:21:14.810 --> 00:21:25.470
The combat system is the core of the game and learning how to attack enemies, but also use the various magic you get is the core gameplay loop.

00:21:25.630 --> 00:21:35.370
And not that that's a bad thing, that there aren't puzzles, but it's worth mentioning that, you know, certainly Zelda games have definitely evolved to the point where puzzles, you know, are like a huge part of the game.

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:47.410
Like going into dungeons and moving rocks into certain patterns to like get by even another game that reminds me a lot of Willow Pokemon has a puzzle element to it.

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:49.290
And Willow never went in that direction.

00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:52.190
And I just wonder, you know, did you guys notice that at all?

00:21:52.250 --> 00:21:53.430
It didn't bother me.

00:21:53.510 --> 00:21:54.810
I'm just it's something I noticed.

00:21:54.810 --> 00:21:56.970
I noticed it.

00:21:57.250 --> 00:22:19.390
I think that there's there's enough variety in the enemy designs and a good enough like control scheme for the combat that I it I buy it that this is just a more action focused game like between the different patterns.

00:22:19.390 --> 00:22:29.210
Like it's not just like, oh, there's another like two by one enemy that just looks a little different and they're going to call it a different enemy.

00:22:29.610 --> 00:22:40.010
They all seem to have their own patterns and like kill conditions and like the magic affects them differently and the magic itself adding a bit to that.

00:22:40.010 --> 00:22:48.790
So I think there's enough there to say that it's just not puzzle focused and not so much like the lack of them.

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:51.550
It like had a hole in the game.

00:22:52.510 --> 00:22:56.370
Yeah, I actually didn't really notice or think about the fact that there are no puzzles.

00:22:56.630 --> 00:23:00.890
I mean, now that you mention it, it's apparent, but I, you know, I love a good puzzle,

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:10.050
but I think I was just having a good enough time with what they offered in this game that I wasn't feeling like it needed to add more like Zelda like dungeon-y puzzles.

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:17.210
And my Pokemon analogy is kind of funny because some people will argue with you whether Pokemon is a JRPG or not.

00:23:17.370 --> 00:23:22.190
But my my thinking of Pokemon is just because that's what I played when I was a kid and getting that feeling of adventure.

00:23:22.190 --> 00:23:31.490
But really JRPGs have always been doing this where they start you in your hometown and you like talk to your folks and they say like, you know, have a, you know, like go on this mission.

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:39.790
And like you talk to the local guy who in Pokemon is Professor Oak, but I don't remember his name here, but he gives you your first sword.

00:23:40.090 --> 00:23:41.010
High Aldwin.

00:23:41.170 --> 00:23:42.650
Oh, no. High Aldwin was one guy.

00:23:42.790 --> 00:23:44.710
Oh, yeah. But he gives you the magic, right?

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:47.550
Like the acorns. Yeah, the acorns.

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:48.930
And then Von Carr gives you the sword.

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:54.930
Right, right. And so, you know, like that whole like trope that always happens in JRPGs.

00:23:55.050 --> 00:24:01.010
It's funny that I think of Pokemon, but really, like I just think even if it's been done before, it's just done really well here.

00:24:01.010 --> 00:24:09.910
And there's like immediate buy-in into the process just by the way the game starts, you know, being so straightforward and being like go north.

00:24:09.910 --> 00:24:15.810
And then you go north and you run into some enemies on particular screens and then you meet the next town.

00:24:15.810 --> 00:24:26.430
And it's like that reminded me of being in Pallet Town and going to Viridian, you know, that's just that's that's where my mind went.

00:24:26.930 --> 00:24:32.270
Yeah, I definitely felt that, too, because it it just feels like such a condensed world.

00:24:32.450 --> 00:24:38.350
Like even back when I was a kid, it was just like, oh, like the next town over is only like three or four screens.

00:24:38.350 --> 00:24:41.930
But yeah, I didn't actually put that together until now.

00:24:42.070 --> 00:24:45.170
But now that you say it, like that's that's just it.

00:24:45.730 --> 00:24:53.330
And I'm not trying to like start a fight here between turn based RPGs and ARPGs because they are completely different genres.

00:24:53.330 --> 00:25:05.250
But I feel like just because we played Dragon Warrior this year as well, this game kind of and I know like in the Japanese like library, Dragon Warrior was a much earlier game.

00:25:05.430 --> 00:25:10.750
But this game kind of like eats Dragon Warrior's lunch just in the terms of ideas that it was setting out, right?

00:25:10.930 --> 00:25:15.050
Going out on an adventure and that sense of exploration.

00:25:15.050 --> 00:25:25.810
Like in Dragon Warrior, you're just walking over in a world map and so you don't really get that sense of like crossing over a desert or being in a forest.

00:25:26.090 --> 00:25:38.390
But, you know, Willow's able to do it a lot more convincingly because you are real time attacking other enemies and still going through those environments, but actually like getting lost in them.

00:25:38.390 --> 00:25:50.650
Yeah, I mean, I think whether whether or not it's it's turn based or action, there's just like there's just a polish to the design of the world of Willow that is more attractive.

00:25:51.390 --> 00:25:56.350
Personally, you know, I mean, it just depends on what you're what you're you know, what you're looking for, I guess.

00:25:56.450 --> 00:26:04.190
But for me, it's just this world that feels like a world, you know, whereas the you know, like Dragon Warrior.

00:26:05.290 --> 00:26:09.030
Maybe feels like a world, but not not as robust as this.

00:26:09.210 --> 00:26:13.550
I mean, you know, that feels like the the elements, the concepts that you want in your world.

00:26:13.550 --> 00:26:15.390
Like, oh, the desert area, the cave, this.

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:17.110
But this feels like everything has been.

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:22.390
I don't know. Everything is much more more intentional feeling.

00:26:22.390 --> 00:26:24.100
There we go.

00:26:26.630 --> 00:26:29.830
There we go.

00:26:35.210 --> 00:26:37.250
There we go.

00:26:59.630 --> 00:27:06.570
When you play every game on the system like we do, and we've now just hit our 250th game,

00:27:07.230 --> 00:27:15.670
you know, I think sometimes developers do stuff that is really cool on paper and, you know,

00:27:15.750 --> 00:27:18.390
like, oh, neat ideas, didn't think they were thinking of this stuff back then,

00:27:18.390 --> 00:27:22.970
but the hardware just wasn't there yet, and we've experienced a lot of cool ideas.

00:27:23.450 --> 00:27:29.750
I'm not arguing that Willow is lame, but it is a very simple game,

00:27:29.910 --> 00:27:38.570
and yet it's a very, it's a long game by NES standards, right, in terms of, like, the story and scope and the world,

00:27:38.570 --> 00:27:51.670
and there's a, even though it's simple and straightforward, the combat system and the magic and experience points that you develop,

00:27:52.230 --> 00:28:00.410
I feel like add and, you know, do enough of a job here that I wasn't really left, like, wanting more.

00:28:00.410 --> 00:28:04.990
I wasn't thinking of, like, oh, well, you know, it would have been cool if they added, like, day and night cycle, right?

00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:06.970
Just stupid stuff like that, but you know what I mean?

00:28:07.030 --> 00:28:11.530
Like, the other things, it was, it never bothered me that the game didn't really evolve.

00:28:12.190 --> 00:28:18.930
Yeah, I think that, like, you don't need to be particularly challenged to the point of, like,

00:28:18.950 --> 00:28:22.770
having to try over and over and over and over again for something to be fun.

00:28:22.770 --> 00:28:31.310
Like, I think the systems there are fun, and all the other, excuse me, all the other tangibles, like,

00:28:31.450 --> 00:28:37.110
just the graphics are, this is a very pretty game, like, the sprites are beautiful.

00:28:38.630 --> 00:28:46.990
The, all those little quality of life touches in the UI that I mentioned before, like, it's just a pleasant game to play,

00:28:46.990 --> 00:28:55.270
and I don't think that, like, incredible difficulty is necessary for it to be considered worthwhile.

00:28:55.630 --> 00:29:02.070
Yeah, I mean, to that point, I think that as far as difficulty of the game, there are elements that are really difficult,

00:29:02.500 --> 00:29:07.190
but it never feels, it never feels ridiculously punishing.

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:09.630
It doesn't feel like it just lets you slide.

00:29:09.630 --> 00:29:13.690
Like, I think they just found a good balance that a lot of NES games haven't found of, like,

00:29:14.630 --> 00:29:18.310
the punishment that sort of supplements the difficulty of it, where it's like,

00:29:18.410 --> 00:29:23.610
if you die, you lose all the experience you've gained since your last level,

00:29:23.970 --> 00:29:27.330
and you go back to, like, the last town or place you healed.

00:29:27.510 --> 00:29:30.890
You don't go back to the beginning of the game, you don't go down a bunch of levels,

00:29:30.930 --> 00:29:36.150
you don't lose any of your items, but you also never feel like it's okay to just die at any time,

00:29:36.150 --> 00:29:38.510
because there is a consequence.

00:29:39.270 --> 00:29:45.390
So, I think that they found, like, a pretty sweet spot compared to, like, some other games.

00:29:46.090 --> 00:29:50.470
Yeah, and even just deaths in general, this is gonna be a weird statement,

00:29:50.670 --> 00:29:52.050
but I understand what I mean.

00:29:52.250 --> 00:29:53.590
You guys let me know if it's confusing.

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:59.030
I died as many times in this game as I die in, like, modern games.

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:01.670
Like, the pacing of death felt right.

00:30:01.790 --> 00:30:06.150
You don't die that often in modern games, unless you're playing, like, you know, on a harder difficulty.

00:30:06.150 --> 00:30:09.490
Like, games are just made a little easier now than they were back then,

00:30:09.490 --> 00:30:14.190
and I feel like when I died, I, you know, I accepted my deaths in Willow,

00:30:14.210 --> 00:30:18.630
because it didn't happen, like, every other screen, or, like, I was severely underleveled.

00:30:18.730 --> 00:30:21.070
Like, it just was like, oh, I wasn't prepared for that challenge,

00:30:21.070 --> 00:30:25.410
and next time I know to either go around those guys, or deal with them differently,

00:30:25.410 --> 00:30:32.450
or make them come to me so I can broad strokes or, what do you, yeah, like, swing the sword wider.

00:30:33.910 --> 00:30:39.870
You know, like, I think that's just, like, a funny idea that this game might have, you know,

00:30:39.910 --> 00:30:43.210
might not have been as intense in difficulty as other games back then,

00:30:43.310 --> 00:30:45.750
but that is now a plus, like, in retrospect.

00:30:46.470 --> 00:30:53.350
Yeah, that wasn't really something I was thinking about, but I, you know, looking back, I think I agree.

00:30:54.510 --> 00:30:59.110
It was, I mean, I wouldn't say, actually, that modern games are just easier.

00:30:59.350 --> 00:31:04.830
Like, at least with this, there is some punishment to dying that's not just, like, in a Naughty Dog game.

00:31:04.830 --> 00:31:08.810
It's like, oh, you last saved 39 seconds ago, or something like that.

00:31:08.810 --> 00:31:21.130
Like, there's still a, you're still setting back if you've died, but it's, you don't die as often as in some of the games we played recently.

00:31:21.430 --> 00:31:25.670
Yeah, I think that, for me, it just, what it came down to is it all felt fair.

00:31:25.670 --> 00:31:30.710
Like, I didn't feel frustrated all the time whenever I would lose, whenever I would die.

00:31:30.710 --> 00:31:31.590
It wasn't frustrating.

00:31:31.770 --> 00:31:35.430
It was like, like you said, Mike, now I gotta go back and try it again, and I got an idea.

00:31:35.430 --> 00:31:41.010
Like, that's kind of, like, how I feel in, like, not even just modern games, even, like, games in, like, the 90s.

00:31:41.270 --> 00:31:45.610
Like, the late 90s, you know, where I'd be like, okay, I died here.

00:31:46.650 --> 00:31:52.430
It's not so frustrating that, like, okay, now I gotta go do all this stuff again, or, you know, I'll never...

00:31:52.890 --> 00:31:56.730
A lot of games that we play, I feel like you go to a boss, and you don't know the thing you need to do.

00:31:57.130 --> 00:32:04.410
And the only way to know it is by, like, somebody telling you, or, like, getting there a million times and taking a ton of time to, like, figure it out.

00:32:04.410 --> 00:32:12.510
Whereas this, there were a couple times I got to a boss, didn't know what I needed to do, like, didn't realize I needed something else, died, and then was like, okay, let's try something else.

00:32:12.530 --> 00:32:16.530
And I went and explored more, and then came back with, like, armed with knowledge.

00:32:16.790 --> 00:32:24.230
And I felt like that that, the game was designed to let you do that, rather than to be like, well, you died, start the whole game over, maybe next time when you get here, you'll know how to do it.

00:32:29.450 --> 00:32:38.110
To get into the combat, I feel like the easiest way to talk about the combat, we've already said up that this is a Zelda game, and that it happens in real time and everything,

00:32:38.370 --> 00:32:47.490
is that there's not combat at all times, and you'll know when there's combat, because when you enter a particular screen, it'll just start, like, the environment will just start shaking.

00:32:48.430 --> 00:32:50.730
And, you know, I don't know about you guys, but that kind of caught me off guard.

00:32:51.530 --> 00:32:55.830
It's a violent kind of shake, and I don't know if it was necessary or not.

00:32:55.830 --> 00:33:02.410
I get it that it communicates that there are enemies coming, but so are the enemies on the screen are communicating to me that there are enemies here.

00:33:03.350 --> 00:33:15.450
I mean, I know there are parts where the screen does shake wholesale, but I also was noticing that it's mostly, like, oh, the grass starts blowing, or, like, the leaves and trees.

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:17.070
It's not the whole screen all the time.

00:33:17.070 --> 00:33:27.890
Right, right, it's like an atmospheric thing, which could work, but I just feel like it happens, it's like they designed two frames of it, you know, like, grass to the left, grass to the right, and it just shakes between those two.

00:33:28.410 --> 00:33:35.570
And maybe I'm being, like, you know, I'm exaggerating the effect, but to me it was just, is this necessary?

00:33:36.490 --> 00:33:38.190
I mean, I can see both sides of it.

00:33:38.230 --> 00:33:47.090
It is a little jarring when you first get to it, and yes, maybe it is definitely not necessary to tell you that there are enemies on the screen, because there are enemies on the screen.

00:33:48.790 --> 00:33:59.570
But I did feel like they were trying to go for, like, this, like, sort of anime style, the battle starts and, like, wind is blowing through the grass, like, you know, like, as you're squaring off with the enemies.

00:33:59.750 --> 00:34:00.690
And I thought that was cool.

00:34:01.310 --> 00:34:11.010
I liked it, but I'm not going to defend it and say it's like, oh, yeah, that was definitely needed, and it definitely, like, fully only exclusively worked.

00:34:11.230 --> 00:34:15.190
I can get people getting annoyed with those two frames of animation.

00:34:15.190 --> 00:34:24.970
And then within combat, you'll have your sword, which you can upgrade several times, and obviously certain swords are stronger than others, but it comes with a, like, a weight to it.

00:34:25.090 --> 00:34:32.010
And obviously there's, the only way to translate that weight is just how fast you can attack in rapid succession.

00:34:32.010 --> 00:34:37.050
So the heavier the sword, the more time it takes to swing that sword.

00:34:37.910 --> 00:34:43.310
You get a shield eventually, and the shield I thought was pretty cool, because I was like, oh, what button do I have to use for the shield?

00:34:43.410 --> 00:34:44.570
And it's like, no buttons.

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:48.030
Like, when you're not doing anything, it's your shield is active.

00:34:48.030 --> 00:34:52.350
And when you're doing anything else, then you can, then your shield won't help you.

00:34:52.890 --> 00:35:03.550
And then the big thing is the spells and the magic, which I feel like is the thing in the game that evolves and spices up the combat gameplay.

00:35:03.950 --> 00:35:09.790
And even early on, I was surprised by, like, some of these spells and the power that they give you.

00:35:09.830 --> 00:35:14.950
And, like, that one that creates the circle of fireballs, I was like, oh, this is op right now.

00:35:15.070 --> 00:35:17.570
Like, I'm going to walk all over enemies whenever I have this.

00:35:17.910 --> 00:35:19.150
Yeah, that was the first one.

00:35:19.470 --> 00:35:27.870
Not exactly did I think it was, like, it was op, but I thought it was very, um, it was just designed uniquely.

00:35:28.070 --> 00:35:35.070
I feel like a lot of times we see magic, and it's like, okay, this is the fireball, and here's the lightning bolt, and they're, like, the same kind of thing, and they're, like, slightly different.

00:35:35.070 --> 00:35:48.390
But it's, like, it feels like most spells not only, like, do a different amount of damage or whatever, but also, like, they have a different effect or, like, a different way you have to think about using it.

00:35:48.410 --> 00:35:49.710
I mean, you mentioned the fire thing.

00:35:49.870 --> 00:35:52.350
That's one of my, that was one of my favorites because it was just so cool.

00:35:52.470 --> 00:35:56.850
You create a ring of fire around where you're standing, and then you use that as, like, a fork.

00:35:57.090 --> 00:35:59.050
You know, you stay in that ring of fire as you're fighting.

00:35:59.490 --> 00:36:00.830
Like, it just makes you think about it differently.

00:36:00.870 --> 00:36:02.630
It's not just shoot a fire projectile.

00:36:02.630 --> 00:36:07.950
You know, that later on, there's, like, a wind thing that, like, is, like, an area of effect thing that shoots in, like, all directions.

00:36:07.950 --> 00:36:11.910
It just felt like each thing was, there was thought put into each spell.

00:36:12.110 --> 00:36:32.610
Right. It might be a stretch here, but I'm even going to suggest that, like, certain things, not every single one of them, but, like, that fire one, for instance, it felt like it was something that you first go through that cave, and there's those annoying bats that, you know, if, depending on where they come from, you can't reach them, and then if you don't attack them quick enough, they turn into, um, I guess, like, the vampire version of themselves.

00:36:32.630 --> 00:36:33.990
Or beast version of themselves.

00:36:34.470 --> 00:36:35.830
It's a bigger bat. Yeah, bigger bat.

00:36:35.930 --> 00:36:37.450
There you go. Couldn't tell what they were going for.

00:36:38.250 --> 00:36:50.090
But then once you, uh, once you do everything in that cave, you get the fire spell, and then when you're doing, uh, that, that thing, it makes it so much easier to deal with the bats, like, specifically.

00:36:50.370 --> 00:36:53.990
So it was, like, a cool instant gratification thing of not a spell that you'll need later.

00:36:53.990 --> 00:37:00.270
It's, like, now you can actually, uh, go through this cave, uh, on the way back out much easier.

00:37:01.010 --> 00:37:05.350
Yeah, I mean, that's, that's, I, that went over my head, but now that you're saying it, it does make sense.

00:37:05.350 --> 00:37:12.070
It seems like it was intentionally designed to show you why you, like, why you didn't realize you needed this spell.

00:37:12.250 --> 00:37:14.350
You know, it's like, oh, the whole time these things are so annoying.

00:37:14.430 --> 00:37:17.070
It's like, oh, this spell's perfect for dealing with these things I was just dealing with.

00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:26.210
Um, in RPGs, people are usually guilty of, you know, like, saving their items, uh, for the end, and, uh, there's not really items like that.

00:37:26.230 --> 00:37:36.090
But the, the magic that you have, especially early on when you don't have much MP, uh, you know, I, at least, was guilty of not using it.

00:37:36.150 --> 00:37:38.450
And it was, like, freeing once I finally was like, no, screw it.

00:37:38.470 --> 00:37:39.590
Like, I'm playing this game.

00:37:39.830 --> 00:37:40.990
I'm probably not gonna beat it.

00:37:41.070 --> 00:37:42.130
Like, I just want to experience it.

00:37:42.130 --> 00:37:46.210
I started using my magic for fights that I just didn't want to deal with anymore.

00:37:46.410 --> 00:37:51.550
And it makes me think just in general about RPGs and how I should probably just use shit more often.

00:37:51.890 --> 00:37:52.850
Um, but I don't know.

00:37:52.910 --> 00:37:56.890
Did you guys, like, conserve your MP, or were you pretty free with it?

00:37:57.530 --> 00:38:05.730
Yeah, there were points when I, because I got a healing spell, and I noticed I wasn't really picking up anything that could heal.

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:12.970
You have to go to, um, a specific house in towns to heal otherwise.

00:38:13.050 --> 00:38:15.410
Or pick up things from treasure.

00:38:15.630 --> 00:38:16.850
It's not a usable item.

00:38:16.990 --> 00:38:18.270
It's just like, here's a treasure chest.

00:38:18.450 --> 00:38:20.430
There's a doctor in there, and he's gonna heal you.

00:38:20.930 --> 00:38:28.550
Um, so, there were points where I was sort of just conserving it to, like, oh, I might need to use the healing spell.

00:38:28.990 --> 00:38:38.190
